# Enrich policy over the LOCAL kibana_sdp_amdb, for the ENRICH variant of the
# consolidated rule (10008_single_stage_enrich_variant).
#
# Run this on the LOCAL / CCS cluster -- the same cluster Kibana and
# kibana_sdp_amdb already live on. Nothing is needed on prod: the rule uses
# ENRICH _coordinator:, which forces the enrich to execute on the coordinating
# (local) cluster, so the policy only has to exist here.
#
# match_field is hostname.keyword, not hostname: in kibana_sdp_amdb, hostname is
# mapped as text with a .keyword subfield, and an enrich match_field has to be a
# keyword. The rule matches it against host.hostname from metricbeat, which is
# already keyword, so the types line up.
#
# enrich_fields is deliberately just the two the rule filters on. Enrich adds its
# fields as new columns and same-named columns get overwritten, so keeping the
# list narrow removes any chance of shadowing a metricbeat column.

PUT _enrich/policy/kibana_sdp_amdb_policy
{
  "match": {
    "indices": "kibana_sdp_amdb",
    "match_field": "hostname.keyword",
    "enrich_fields": [
      "group",
      "alert_status"
    ]
  }
}

# An enrich policy is a POINT-IN-TIME SNAPSHOT. It does nothing until executed,
# and it does not track later changes to kibana_sdp_amdb -- this is the main
# functional difference from LOOKUP JOIN, which always reads the live index.
# Execute it once now:

PUT _enrich/policy/kibana_sdp_amdb_policy/_execute

# ...and re-execute it whenever AMDB config changes (a host is added, or an
# alert is enabled/disabled for a host). Until you do, the rule keeps filtering
# against the config as it was at the last execution: newly enabled hosts will
# not alert, and newly disabled hosts will keep alerting. Wire the re-execution
# into whatever maintains kibana_sdp_amdb, or schedule it (hourly/daily is
# plenty for config that changes rarely) -- do not leave it manual.
#
# Inspect the policy and confirm it has been executed:

GET _enrich/policy/kibana_sdp_amdb_policy
GET _enrich/_stats

# Executing a policy builds a hidden .enrich-* system index; it is rebuilt on
# each execution and the old one is cleaned up. To remove the policy entirely:
#
#   DELETE _enrich/policy/kibana_sdp_amdb_policy
